{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012vZgJm13evNzFMPCqgWg1k","type":"tool_result","content":"sed: 1: \"scanners/docs/docscan.go\n\": bad flag in substitute command: 's'\nsed: 1: \"config.yaml\n\": command c expects \\ followed by text\nsed: -I or -i may not be used with stdin\nconfig.yaml:2:# Anything unset takes its built-in default (docs/proposal.md, Configuration).\n../../CLAUDE.md:54:internal/roles/     starter role directories embedded for ard init (scanners/cve, triage/lead, triage/engineer, workers/engineer, reviewers/eng-review)\n../../docs/proposal.md:211:**The cve scanner** (`scanners/cve`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program, no build step) merges osv-scanner (all ecosystems) and govulncheck (Go reachability). Its fingerprint is `osv:<advisory>:<manifest>:<package>`, where the advisory is the smallest `GHSA-` id of the vulnerability, else the smallest `CVE-` id, else the smallest id, so one vulnerability reported under `GO-`, `GHSA-` and `CVE-` ids is one finding. Severity is `critical|high|medium|low`, from the CVSS score, else the database severity, else `medium`; a vulnerability govulncheck finds unreachable is capped at `medium`. Manifests under `testdata/`, `vendor/` and `node_modules/` are skipped. It fails closed: if a Go module cannot be analysed, the run fails and files nothing, unless a human adds a `no-govulncheck` file to the role directory, which skips govulncheck and reports reachability as unknown. Each Go module is analysed with the image's Go toolchain unless its `go` directive, or a `toolchain` line, needs a newer one. Then the scanner lets Go pick that toolchain (`GOTOOLCHAIN=auto` rules: the `toolchain` line if present, else the `go` directive's minimum version), downloads it from the Go module proxy (checksum-verified; scanner jobs have egress) into its temp dir, rebuilds the same govulncheck version with it, and scans with both. The image sets `TMPDIR=/var/tmp` because the executor's `/tmp` is noexec. If the temp dir is noexec, or the toolchain cannot be downloaded or built, the run fails closed and names the reason. Findings from such a module say which toolchain was scanned (`Go toolchain:` line), and standard-library findings are measured against it (for `go 1.27`, `go1.27.0`), not against the image's Go. The Dependabot alerts API is not wrapped, because a scanner holds no token.\n../../docs/proposal.md:403:  scanners/cve:             { every: 6h, max_open: 10, group_size: 1, autoclose: true }\n../../docs/proposal.md:417:**`ard init` proposes the starter `.ard/` as a PR (decided, #27).** It builds `config.yaml` and the starter roles in memory (today `scanners/cve`, `triage/lead`, `triage/engineer`, `workers/engineer` and `reviewers/eng-review`; `intake: {mode: label}`, the protected-path defaults above, and the cve, lead, engineer, worker and eng-review overrides), compares them with `main`, commits them on a new branch `ard-init` cut from `main` through the effect layer and a narrow committer lane, pushes it and opens a PR. It writes nothing to the local working tree or to `main`, and `scan` is committed `100755`. Files that already exist on `main` with different contents are a conflict naming the paths; `--force` proposes overwriting them, and init never deletes anything. It refuses while `ard-init` exists on the remote, whatever its PR's state, so it never force-pushes over or stacks commits on a human's edits: merge or close the PR, delete the branch and rerun. If `main` already has every starter file it prints `nothing to propose`. It syncs the label catalogue first (like `ard labels sync`), so a failure leaves nothing pushed. With `--dry-run` the commit, push and PR are logged to the ledger instead of acting. A missing repo, credentials or `main` branch exits 1 with nothing written, and `ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN` auth is refused outside `--dry-run`. The PR body carries the one step the App cannot do: copy `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` and set the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` (optionally `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`). It also says to merge with **Squash and merge** or **Create a merge commit**, never a fast-forward from the command line, which would put the bot's commit on main's first-parent history. **Rebase and merge** is also safe: the scratch-repo run (#85) showed GitHub records the merging human as committer of a rebase-merged commit (the bot stays the author), and the bot-commit check keys on committer, so it does not flag it. The body still names only the two merges above.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7509fb23-cc48-478a-86a3-3a969af8b5b2","uuid":"9f3448ef-892c-4339-8898-b521b88d1b14","timestamp":"2026-11-23T16:00:40.267Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"sed: 1: \"scanners/docs/docscan.go\n\": bad flag in substitute command: 's'\nsed: 1: \"config.yaml\n\": command c expects \\ followed by text\nsed: -I or -i may not be used with stdin\nconfig.yaml:2:# Anything unset takes its built-in default (docs/proposal.md, Configuration).\n../../CLAUDE.md:54:internal/roles/     starter role directories embedded for ard init (scanners/cve, triage/lead, triage/engineer, workers/engineer, reviewers/eng-review)\n../../docs/proposal.md:211:**The cve scanner** (`scanners/cve`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program, no build step) merges osv-scanner (all ecosystems) and govulncheck (Go reachability). Its fingerprint is `osv:<advisory>:<manifest>:<package>`, where the advisory is the smallest `GHSA-` id of the vulnerability, else the smallest `CVE-` id, else the smallest id, so one vulnerability reported under `GO-`, `GHSA-` and `CVE-` ids is one finding. Severity is `critical|high|medium|low`, from the CVSS score, else the database severity, else `medium`; a vulnerability govulncheck finds unreachable is capped at `medium`. Manifests under `testdata/`, `vendor/` and `node_modules/` are skipped. It fails closed: if a Go module cannot be analysed, the run fails and files nothing, unless a human adds